:"ruler","value_qid":"Q3462649","value_label":"Min Aung Hlaing","from_t":2024.5546448087432,"to_t":null,"title":"President of Myanmar","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Min Aung Hlaing"},{"qid":"Q836","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q422413","value_label":"Win Maung","from_t":1957,"to_t":1962.164383561644,"title":"President of Myanmar","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Win Maung"},{"qid":"Q836","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q586850","value_label":"Maung Maung","from_t":1988.6311475409836,"to_t":1988.7131147540983,"title":"President of Myanmar","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Maung Maung"},{"qid":"Q836","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q63697","value_label":"San Yu","from_t":1981.854794520548,"to_t":1988.568306010929,"title":"President of Myanmar","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"San Yu"},{"qid":"Q836","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q8023642","value_label":"Win Myint","from_t":2021.2876712328766,"to_t":null,"title":"President of Myanmar","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Win Myint"},{"qid":"Q836","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q8023642","value_label":"Win Myint","from_t":2018.2410958904109,"to_t":2021.0849315068492,"title":"President of Myanmar","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Win Myint"},{"qid":"Q836","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q3331362","value_label":"Myint Swe","from_t":2021.0849315068492,"to_t":2024.5546448087432,"title":"President of Myanmar","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Myint Swe"},{"qid":"Q836","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q163753","value_label":"Flag of Myanmar.svg","from_t":2010.8027397260273,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Myanmar","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Myanmar"}],"people":{"Q3331362":{"label":"Myint Swe","description":"Army officer and former Acting President of Myanmar (1951–2025)","enwiki":"Myint Swe","summary":"Myint Swe (24 June 1951 – 7 August 2025) was a Burmese politician and army officer who served as the third first vice president of Myanmar from 2016 until his death in 2025. A member of the Union Solidarity and Development Party (USDP), Myint Swe served as acting president of Myanmar in 2018 and again from 2021 to 2025.\nMyint Swe was installed as acting president by the Tatmadaw through a coup d'état on 1 February 2021, after which he immediately declared a state of emergency and formally transferred power to coup leader Min Aung Hlaing. Throughout his political career, Myint Swe worked to ensure the Tatmadaw's influence in politics.","image":"Myint Swe (2018-12-13) (cropped).jpg","birth":1951.3917808219178,"death":2025.5972602739726},"Q3462649":{"label":"Min Aung Hlaing","description":"11th President of Myanmar","enwiki":"Min Aung Hlaing","summary":"Min Aung Hlaing (born 3 July 1956) is a Burmese politician and retired military officer who has been the 11th president of Myanmar since April 2026, having ruled the country continuously since seizing power in February 2021 through a military coup d'état against the elected government. Prior to the start of his nominally civilian presidency, he was a military ruler under various titles. He was appointed the commander-in-chief of Defence Services—the leader of the Tatmadaw—in 2011 and remained in that post through the period of military rule, until stepping down in 2026 to become the president.\nBorn in Minbu, Min Aung Hlaing studied law at the Rangoon Arts and Science University before joining the military.","image":"President Min Aung Hlaing 2026 (cropped).jpg","birth":1956.5027322404371,"death":null},"Q12149":{"label":"Thein Sein","description":"former President and Prime Minister of Myanmar","enwiki":"Thein Sein","summary":"Thein Sein (Burmese: သိန်းစိန်; IPA: [θéɪɰ̃ sèɪɰ̃]; born 20 April 1945) is a Burmese politician and retired army general who served as the eighth president of Myanmar from 2011 to 2016. He previously served as 11th prime minister from 2007 to 2010, and was considered by many in and outside Myanmar as a reformist leader in the post-junta government.\nHis government undertook a series of political reforms including some deregulation of the country's censored media, releasing many political prisoners and halting the country's controversial large Chinese-led hydro-power project. The developments that followed included Myanmar's appointment to chair ASEAN in 2014, improved relations with the United States, the release of Aung San Suu Kyi – his 2015 general election rival – from house arrest, and the reinstatement of major opposition party National League for Democracy (NLD) in the by-election held on 1 April 2012.","image":"Thein Sein.jpg","birth":1945.2986301369863,"death":null},"Q202553":{"label":"Than Shwe","description":"Burmese senior general and head of state of Myanmar of Myanmar from 1992 to 2011","enwiki":"Than Shwe","summary":"Than Shwe (Burmese: သန်းရွှေ; pronounced [θáɰ̃ ʃwè]; born 2 February 1933) is a retired Burmese military officer who ruled Myanmar as the second chairman of the State Law and Order Restoration Council, later as the only chairman of the State Peace and Development Council, as well as the commander-in-chief of Defence Services from 1992 until his resignation in 2011, and concurrently served as the eighth prime minister of Myanmar from 1992 to 2003. He previously served as the deputy commander-in-chief of Defence Services from 1985, and the vice chairman of the State Peace and Development Council, and deputy prime minister of Myanmar from 1988 until 1992.\nBorn in Kyaukse in Upper Burma, Than Shwe received military training and in 1953 joined the Tatmadaw (armed forces), and rose through the ranks. In 1985, he was appointed the deputy commander-in-chief of Defence Services.","image":"Than Shwe 2010-10-11.jpg","birth":1933.0876712328768,"death":null},"Q23020990":{"label":"Htin Kyaw","description":"Burmese politician and scholar serving as the 9th President of Myanmar","enwiki":"Htin Kyaw","summary":"Htin Kyaw (Burmese: ထင်ကျော်, pronounced [tʰɪ̀ɴ tɕɔ̀] or [tʰɪ̀ɴ dʑɔ̀]; born 20 July 1946) is a Burmese politician, writer and scholar who served as the ninth president of Myanmar from 2016 until his resignation in 2018. He was the first elected president to hold the office with no ties to the military since the 1962 coup d'état. The second son of scholar Min Thu Wun, Htin Kyaw had held various positions in the education, planning and treasury ministries in prior governments.\nThe ethnic Mon-Bamar politician is viewed as an important ally of the National League for Democracy (NLD) leader and State Counsellor Aung San Suu Kyi, who is constitutionally barred from the presidency.","image":"President U Htin Kyaw official.jpg","birth":1946.5479452054794,"death":null},"Q2877623":{"label":"Ba U","description":"2nd President of Burma","enwiki":"Ba U","summary":"Sir Ba U (Burmese: ဘဦး, pronounced [ba̰ ʔú]; 26 May 1887 – 9 November 1963), was an Anglo-Burmese politician and lawyer. He served as Chief Justice of the Supreme Court of Burma from 1948 to 1952, and the second president of Burma from 16 March 1952 to 13 March 1957.","image":"Ba U.JPG","birth":1887.3972602739725,"death":1963.854794520548},"Q3034878":{"label":"Sao Shwe Thaik","description":"First President of Burma and Last Saopha of Yawnghwe","enwiki":"Sao Shwe Thaik","summary":"Sao Shwe Thaik (Shan: ၸဝ်ႈၶမ်းသိူၵ်ႈ, Tsaw³ Kham⁴soek³; Burmese: စဝ်ရွှေသိုက်, Burmese pronunciation: [saʔ ʃwè θaiʔ]; 16 October 1895 – 21 November 1962) was a Burmese politician who served as the first president of the Union of Burma and the last Saopha of Yawnghwe. His full royal title was Kambawsarahta Thiri Pawaramahawuntha Thudamaraza. He was a well-respected Shan political figure in Burma. His residence in Nyaung Shwe (Yawnghwe), the Haw, is now the \"Buddha Museum\" and is open to the public.","image":"Sao Shwe Thaik.jpg","birth":1896.7896174863388,"death":1962.8876712328768},"Q315522":{"label":"Ne Win","description":"Military leader and head of state of Burma from 1962 to 1988","enwiki":"Ne Win","summary":"Ne Win (born Shu Maung; 24 May 1911 – 5 December 2002) was a Burmese general and politician who served as Burma's head of government from 1958 to 1960 and again from 1962 to 1974; and also as head of state from 1962 to 1981. Ne Win was Burma's military dictator during the Socialist Republic of the Union of Burma period of 1962 to 1988.\nNe Win founded the Burma Socialist Programme Party (BSPP) and overthrew the democratic Union Parliament of U Nu in the 1962 Burmese coup d'état, establishing Burma as a one-party socialist state under the Burmese Way to Socialism ideology. Ne Win was Burma's de facto leader as chairman of the BSPP, serving in various official titles as part of his military government, and was known by his supporters as U Ne Win.","image":"Ne Win in 1959, Jerusalem.jpg","birth":1911.3917808219178,"death":2002.9260273972602},"Q422413":{"label":"Win Maung","description":"President of Burma (1916-1989)","enwiki":"Win Maung","summary":"Mahn Win Maung (Burmese: မန်းဝင်းမောင် [máɰ̃ wɪ́ɰ̃ màʊɰ̃]; 17 April 1916 – 4 July 1989) was an Anglo-Burmese politician who served as the third president of Burma.","image":"Mahn Win Maung.jpg","birth":1916.292349726776,"death":1989.504109589041},"Q586850":{"label":"Maung Maung","description":"President of Union of Myanmar (1925-1994)","enwiki":"Maung Maung","summary":"Maung Maung (Burmese: မောင်မောင် [màʊɰ̃ màʊɰ̃]; 31 January 1925 – 2 July 1994), known honorifically as Dr. Maung Maung, was a well-known writer and legal scholar in Myanmar, who served as the seventh president of Burma from 19 August 1988 to 18 September 1988.","image":null,"birth":1925.027397260274,"death":1994.7123287671234},"Q63697":{"label":"San Yu","description":"Burmese general (1918–1996)","enwiki":"San Yu","summary":"San Yu (Burmese: စန်းယု, pronounced [sáɰ̃ jṵ]; 3 March 1918 – 28 January 1996) was a Burmese army general and statesman who served as the fifth president of Myanmar from 9 November 1981 to 27 July 1988.","image":"San Yu.jpg","birth":1918.1671232876713,"death":1996.0792349726776},"Q8023642":{"label":"Win Myint","description":"10th President of Myanmar","enwiki":"Win Myint","summary":"Win Myint (born 8 November 1951), commonly known as U Win Myint, is a Burmese politician and diplomat who served as the tenth president of Myanmar from 2018 to 2021, when he was removed from office in the 2021 coup d'état. He was the speaker of the House of Representatives from 2016 to 2018. He also served as a member of parliament in the House of Representatives (Pyithu Hluttaw) from 2012 to 2018. Win Myint was viewed as an important ally of State Counsellor Aung San Suu Kyi.","image":"Win Myint 2017 cropped.jpg","birth":1951.8520547945207,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Myanmar.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Flag_of_Myanmar.svg/330px-Flag_of_Myanmar.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Myanmar.svg","author":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain"},"State Seal of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/State_Seal_of_the_Republic_of_the_Union_of_Myanmar.svg/330px-State_Seal_of_the_Republic_of_the_Union_of_Myanmar.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_Seal_of_the_Republic_of_the_Union_of_Myanmar.svg","author":"File:State seal of Myanmar.png: Unknown authorUnknown author\nderivative work: Pho Sai","license":"Public domain"},"Myint Swe (2018-12-13) (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Myint_Swe_%282018-12-13%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Myint_Swe_%282018-12-13%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myint_Swe_(2018-12-13)_(cropped).j